---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const card = 'rounded-2xl bg-white border border-neutral-200 p-6 mb-6';
const p = 'text-sm text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed mb-4 last:mb-0';
const h2 = 'font-display text-xl font-bold text-league-red mb-4';
const caption = 'text-xs text-neutral-500 italic mt-2';
---
<Layout title="History">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold mb-2">History of JPL Softball</h1>
    <p class="text-neutral-600 mb-8">Five decades of JPL intramural softball, from one league on Caltech's field to today's B and C Leagues.</p>

    <div class={card}>
      <p class={p}>
        <a class="text-league-red hover:underline" href="#mcnevin">Ed McNevin</a> started the first JPL slo-pitch softball league in 1968, and formally founded the JPL intramural softball program under the auspices of the (now defunct) JPL Employees Recreation Club (ERC) in 1970.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Originally there was only one league — no A, B, or C — with 6 teams, including one from the Caltech Biology Department, playing at Caltech's field. Caltech soon formed its own intramural league, and the JPL league moved to other venues, its teams still mostly organized around JPL departments. Wanting a less competitive, more inclusive coed program, Ed started the B League around 1975, then the casual, highly coed C League around 1979 — open to almost anyone who wanted to play. At JPL softball's peak in the late 1980s / early 1990s, offshoots including B Minor, Coed, and D Leagues also existed, and the program reached roughly 60 teams altogether.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Ed remained the sole commissioner of every JPL league through the mid-1980s, when the program had grown too large for one person, and he began delegating to individual league commissioners. The 1991 season marked the official start of today's JPL Softball Program, with formal Bylaws and Program Rules and a governing council of officers. Scott Morgan has served as overall Program Commissioner since 1992.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        By the mid-1990s, A League could no longer draw enough teams for its top competitive tier, and the various B/C offshoots fell away too. The program consolidated into the moderately competitive B League and the non-competitive C League. Starting in 1996, B League added a short 10-week Fall season after Labor Day; the next year it split its traditional spring/summer season into separate Spring and Summer seasons of about the same length. C League has kept its traditional single ~18-week season, running from mid-April to the end of August, the whole time.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Richard Chen launched the Program's original website in the mid-1990s, and online archives exist for every B and C League season since. Records from the two and a half decades before that exist only on paper.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div id="mcnevin" class={card}>
      <h2 class={h2}>Ed McNevin, Founder of the JPL Softball Leagues</h2>

      <div class="sm:float-left sm:mr-6 sm:mb-2 mb-4 w-full sm:w-56">
        <img src="/history/ted004.jpg" alt="Ed McNevin, circa 1992" class="rounded-xl w-full" />
        <p class={caption}>Ed circa 1992, at his 25th JPL Service Award Ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <p class={p}>Ed McNevin founded the JPL softball program, and singlehandedly managed all of its leagues for the first two decades.</p>
      <p class={p}>
        Edward Donald McNevin, Jr. ("Ed", or "Ted" to his closest friends) was born in Brooklyn, New York, in 1938.
        He came to JPL in 1965 as a contractor and became a regular employee in 1967, arriving with his wife Ruth, who also worked at JPL for a while.
        Always a sports fan and participant, Ed started an informal JPL slo-pitch softball program in 1968, then formally founded the JPL intramural softball program under the auspices of JPL's Employees Recreation Club (ERC) in 1970.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Originally there was only one league — no letters required — with 6 teams, including one from the Caltech Biology Department, playing at Caltech's field. Caltech soon formed its own intramural league (credit due to Ed), and the JPL league moved to other venues with teams still mostly organized by department.
        Wanting a less competitive, more inclusive coed program, Ed started the B League around 1975, then the casual, highly coed C League around 1979, open to almost anyone who wanted to play.
      </p>

      <div class="sm:float-right sm:ml-6 mb-4 w-full sm:w-72 clear-left">
        <img src="/history/TEDAleague86.jpg" alt="Ed's A League team, circa 1986" class="rounded-xl w-full" />
        <p class={caption}>Ed's A League team circa 1986. Ed is in the back row, third from left. His son Skip is the tall fellow in the front row, second from right.</p>
      </div>
      <p class={p}>
        For many years Ed singlehandedly ran the entire softball program while also playing in the A League and the C League, and managing a C League team. His C League team took the name of the original team, the Greezballs; later, when Section 152 became Section 514, it was renamed the P-51s.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Over the years, A League appeared and disappeared depending on whether at least four teams could be found (Ed's rule), and its organizational basis eventually weakened. For a few years in the 1980s there was also a coed D League, heavily organizational, with some JPL Projects and Divisions fielding their own teams.
      </p>

      <div class="sm:float-left sm:mr-6 mb-4 w-24 clear-both">
        <img src="/history/greezballslogo.jpg" alt="The Greezballs team logo" class="rounded-xl w-full" />
        <p class={caption}>The Greezballs logo, on a team T-shirt — one of three logos the team used.</p>
      </div>
      <p class={p}>
        C League was particularly successful, reaching 30 teams at its maximum, each carrying more players than the more competitive leagues — typically 20–25 players. Hundreds of JPL employees, contractors, and family members took part. By the mid-1980s, the program had grown so large that Ed had to delegate some authority to a League Council of officers, league commissioners, and team managers.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Ed's favorite result in C League was a tie game — if his team got too far ahead, he'd send out a deliberately unusual lineup and defensive alignment. This was before he instituted C League's novel mercy rule, which lets play continue while handicapping the stronger team from running up the score any further.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Ed's family took part too. Ruth brought their first son Skip (born 1966) to watch Ed play in the original league, and pitched for the C League team herself for many years, aside from maternity leaves. When Skip was old enough he played on his parents' C League team, and later alongside his father in A League — always trying to chase his dad off of shortstop. Skip went on to work at JPL and served on the League Council, notably as chairman of the one-person Fields Committee, tasked with finding fields for every team. Their younger son Chris (born 1973) also played on his parents' C League team.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6 mb-4 clear-both">
        <div class="sm:w-1/2">
          <img src="/history/75ERC-Honor-Roll.jpg" alt="Ed's induction into the ERC Honor Roll, 1975" class="rounded-xl w-full" />
          <p class={caption}>Ed's induction into the ERC Honor Roll, 1975.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sm:w-1/2">
          <img src="/history/tedplaque86.jpg" alt="Ed's plaque from ERC, 1986" class="rounded-xl w-full" />
          <p class={caption}>Ed's plaque from ERC, 1986.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class={p}>
        Ed was recognized for his contributions throughout: inducted into the ERC Honor Roll in 1975, honored by the Softball Program in 1984 for his years as Commissioner, and presented with a plaque by ERC in 1986. The softball program he built has been a lasting success — both the B League and the C League continue to this day.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Ed played softball through 1993, when he became seriously ill. His beloved family, many friends, and the JPL Softball Program lost Ed on December 8, 1997.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class={card}>
      <h2 class={h2}>B League</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        Dennis Wittman, a longtime veteran of JPL's A, B, C, and D Leagues, recalls the early days: he played for the contractor team "Informatics" in A League starting around 1973, then co-founded "The Ringers" in 1978 after becoming a JPL employee — a team allowed extra non-JPL players to keep the league's team count even. As A League shrank from 8 teams to 3, those teams dropped into B League. Wittman later moved to IPAC and its team, the IR-Sox, which started in C League around 1986 before eventually merging with the Ringers and moving to D League, likely around 1990.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class={card}>
      <h2 class={h2}>C League</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        C League had the highest female-participation requirement of any JPL league at its founding, and was the only fully non-competitive league — no standings kept. It peaked at 30 teams in 1987–88 (a mid-season co-op team briefly pushed it to an all-time record of 31), each carrying 20–25 players; participation has since stabilized at 12–15 teams in the 21st century.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Ed McNevin ran C League himself through 1986. Starting in 1987 he handed it to co-commissioners Sam Dolinar and Ray Morris; Ray continued alone after Sam became the Program's treasurer in 1991. Charlie Kaczinski has served as C League's commissioner ever since taking over a few years later.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class={card}>
      <h2 class={h2}>D League</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        D League grew out of a friendly rivalry between two JPL division managers ("my people can beat your people"), starting as single-organization teams playing for bragging rights before other organizations joined in. Its first season was around 1988, growing to 10 teams by 1990, but it declined through the 1990s and played its final season in 1997 with just 4 teams — an interleague schedule with C League that summer. At least three of those teams, including the Infra Red Sox and the team that became the Eclectic Eccentrics, found a permanent home in C League the following season.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        A League, B Minor League, and Coed League also existed during softball's peak years, but no historical write-up is currently available for them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-neutral-50 border border-neutral-200 p-4 text-sm text-neutral-600">
      Long-time veterans of JPL Softball are encouraged to contact the program (see <a class="text-league-red hover:underline" href="/contact">Contact</a>) with any old records, photos, or scans — they help fill in the program's history prior to the mid-1990s.
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
